#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Checks a Claude Code build against the contract silta-session and silta-claude rely on.

Claude Code has no stable API for a host like Silta: the supervisor speaks its stream-json
protocol, the plugin speaks MCP with a Claude-specific channel extension, and the
deployment rests on a set of flags, environment variables, managed settings and file
locations. Every one of them can change between Claude Code releases, so before a
release is pinned for the minds (`TESTED_VERSIONS` in crates/silta-session/src/contract.rs)
this script runs it once against everything Silta assumes of it, with real turns.

It replaces nothing in the deployment and touches no mind: it runs Claude Code with a
state directory, a workspace and a credential of its own, plays the daemon itself on a
private socket, and lets the real silta-claude plugin connect to that. What it checks is
organised by the piece of Silta that depends on it:

  the supervisor's launch     the command line of `silta-session` (lib.rs `run_once`), the
                              environment knobs Claude Code reads, stdin EOF as the graceful
                              stop (in the middle of a turn, and with a background command
                              running), `--resume` and its failure text, the transcript's path
  the supervisor's reading    every stream-json line `stream.rs` parses: `system init`,
                              main-line `assistant` usage and the `parent_tool_use_id` that
                              sets a subagent's lines apart, `user` and `result` lines with
                              their channel `origin`, `background_tasks_changed`, the
                              `/compact` exchange, `model_fallback`, the `<synthetic>` marker
                              of an API failure, the `control_response` of the window check
                              (at a fresh start and at a resume)
  the rotation                the handoff note written into the memory directory, the
                              PreCompact hook blocking an automatic compaction and leaving
                              the marker (deploy/silta-precompact-block)
  the channel                 the plugin installed from the marketplace as `silta-session-add`
                              does it, registered through `--channels`, its tools in the
                              session, the channel handler Claude Code attaches by the end of
                              the first turn (the plugin's MCP log says when), messages from
                              the daemon becoming `<channel>` user messages with every
                              attribute the plugin sets (a thread, a quote, a file, a
                              reaction), whether they arrive between turns, during a turn or
                              during `/compact`, and turns that answer them with the reply
                              tool
  the managed settings        the keys the deployment sets, and the effect of those a run can
                              see: the channel allowlist and the hook. That the Bash sandbox
                              hides the credential is not checked: a turn that looks for it
                              is refused by auto mode's classifier as credential
                              exploration, and gets the session flagged by the model's
                              safeguards

Two ways to run it:

  on a VM with Silta deployed   `systemctl start silta-contract-check`, then
                                `journalctl -u silta-contract-check`. The unit runs this
                                script as the user silta-check with /var/lib/silta/check as
                                HOME and the credential from /etc/silta/auth/check, laid out
                                like a mind's (oauth-token or api-key). The model, window and
                                the other knobs come from the installed
                                silta-session@.service.
  on a developer's machine      deploy/silta-contract-check from the repository, with the
                                developer's own `claude` and login: the state lives in a
                                temporary directory, the marketplace is registered under the
                                developer's ~/.claude for the duration of the run and removed
                                afterwards (unless it was there already), and the run's
                                transcript and MCP logs are removed at the end.

Every check prints one line, PASS / FAIL / SKIP / WARN / INFO (coloured on a terminal; with
syslog priority prefixes under systemd, so `journalctl -p warning -u silta-contract-check`
lists the failures and warnings alone; `--quiet` leaves PASS and INFO out), and the exit
code is 0 when nothing failed. The whole trace of each run (Claude Code's stdout and stderr,
what was written to its stdin, what the fake daemon saw, the files looked at) is saved as
one JSON line per event under the logs directory; `--replay DIR` runs the same assertions
over a saved trace, for a failure to be examined offline and for CI to keep the assertions
themselves working (dev/contract-fixtures).

In the code, every contract point is one `check_*` function whose docstring says what
Claude Code must do, which part of Silta rests on it and what breaks otherwise; the
`check_static`, `check_main`, `check_resume`, `check_hook`, `check_fallback` and
`check_badkey` functions list them in the order the runs produce the evidence, and the
`drive_*` functions next to them run Claude Code to produce it. The report line of a check
carries the same words as the function's name, so a failure leads straight to its code.

The script is formatted with black at 100 columns and checked with pycodestyle (E203 and
W503 ignored, as black's style needs), both pinned by hash in
dev/contract-check-format-requirements.txt and run by CI in a job of their own, next to the
replay's.

A full run makes some fifteen short turns on the configured model and, for the hook,
two turns on a cheap model over a context of about 106 000 tokens (`--hook-model`;
`--skip hook` leaves that run out); it takes several minutes and costs about a dollar.
The report ends with the run's cost as Claude Code counted it, at list prices, which is
notional on a subscription.
"""

